Alright, I've just introduced a new `TodoItem` type in `src/core/tools.ts` to represent tasks that the system can surface to the user. This addition is part of a broader feature where the CLI can emit a list of TODOs (e.g., “run this command”, “verify this file”) instead of just printing raw text. 

Now that I have a concrete type for TODOs, I need a way for the rest of the codebase—specifically the `buildTools` factory—to accept a callback that will receive an array of these items. Right now `buildTools` only takes an options object with a single `onSubagent` callback. The signature looks like this:

```ts
export function buildTools(opts: { onSubagent?: (task: string) => Promise<string> }): Map<string, ToolSpec> {
  const tools = new Map<string, ToolSpec>();
  const add = (t: ToolSpec) => tools.set(t.name, t);
  // …
}
```

If I simply add a new property to the existing anonymous options type, I’d have to repeat the whole type definition each time I reference it, which would be messy and error‑prone. A cleaner approach is to define a dedicated `BuildToolsOptions` interface that captures both the existing `onSubagent` callback and the new `onTodos` callback. The `onTodos` callback should accept an array of `TodoItem` objects and return `void` (or possibly a Promise, but for now `void` suffices because the caller can handle async work internally if needed). 

I also need to make sure I don’t unintentionally overwrite any other parts of the file. The edit should be scoped to just the function signature and the new interface definition. Using `replace_all: false` ensures I only replace the exact snippet I’m targeting, preserving the rest of the file (including the `ToolSpec` definition and any existing imports). 

So the plan:
1. Insert a new `BuildToolsOptions` interface before the `buildTools` function. This interface will have:
   - `onSubagent?: (task: string) => Promise<string>;` (unchanged)
   - `onTodos?: (todos: TodoItem[]) => void;` (new)
2. Update the `buildTools` function signature to accept `opts: BuildToolsOptions` instead of the anonymous object.
3. Keep the internal implementation (`const tools = new Map...` and `const add = ...`) exactly as before.

The old snippet to replace is the current function header and opening brace:

```ts
